\section{Revisit a Header After the First Iteration}

A diamond exposes a late incoming edge, but a loop additionally tests
whether the solver reopens a branch reached on an earlier visit. Consider
a pure SSA loop with entry carrying $0$ into a header merge $i$.
When $i=0$ the header takes a body edge and returns to the header
carrying $1$; otherwise it exits and returns $i$. There are no
alternative exits or arithmetic traps. In this example the SCCP
domain must establish that both header edges may execute, but it
need not prove that the exit value is $1$. Keep edge activation and
state promotion distinct even when both happen in one queue tick.

\begin{sudocode}
\Require Active-edge bits $E$ and current values $V$
\ForAll{incoming edges $e$ newly activated at header $H$}
  \State Enqueue the merges of $H$
\EndFor
\State When processing the merge for $i$:
\To{Ignore the latch until the latch edge has evidence.}
\Let{x}{\operatorname{joinActiveOperands}(i,E,V)}
\If{$V[i]\ne V[i]\sqcup x$}
  \Let{V[i]}{V[i]\sqcup x}
  \To{An already active branch is still a dependent.}
  \State Enqueue executable users of $i$
\EndIf
\State Process any revised condition to mark additional edges
\end{sudocode}

With the entry edge installed first, the merge becomes $C(0)$
and the branch marks only the body edge. The body then activates
the latch edge carrying $C(1)$. The recomputed merge rises to
$\top$, the equality condition rises to $\top$, and the exit edge
is marked. Concrete execution does one trip and returns $1$.
A solver that processes the header only when it first becomes
reachable reports the exit as dead; a solver that erases the
body edge on the new visit forgets the very input that caused
promotion. Test both the final edge bits and the varying
merge, not only the presence of a return instruction.

Activation is monotone, so each edge is added at most once.
Each value can rise from unseen to constant to varying, with
at most two promotions. Scheduling only changes provides a
finite checkpoint for the solve, though rescanning a large
merge at each event may still be costly. Do not expect the
solver to infer that $i\ne0$ specifically on the exit:
that requires a separate edge refinement or a richer domain.

\section{Nonexecution Is Not Low Frequency}

For a production IR, check the arithmetic width, traps, poison, and exceptional successors explicitly. A division by zero is not an ordinary constant result; a fold that drops an operation must preserve its observable behavior under the dialect's rules. Nor does def-use sparsity make this solver relational. It can lose the pairing between two values selected by the same predicate and therefore miss an expression that is constant on every concrete path.

Roy and Srikant's \emph{The Hot Path SSA Form} explores a speculative counterpart that focuses on frequent reaching definitions. A rare edge omitted from that analysis is not the same as an edge ruled out by SCCP. For instance, a profile containing only left-arm observations says nothing definitive about the next input. Using the resulting constant specialization requires a guard and a correct fallback for the right arm. The dossier's Chapters~3 and~4, \emph{Speculative IR} and \emph{Deoptimization as an IR Operation}, place that fallback in the semantics instead of allowing profile counts to stand in for proofs.
